<h2>What Is Gangnam, Really?</h2>
<p>Gangnam literally means "south of the river" — it's the collective name for the upscale districts south of the Han River that exploded in development during Korea's economic miracle of the 1970s and 80s. Today it's synonymous with wealth, plastic surgery clinics, luxury brands, and a kind of aggressive ambition that feels distinctly Korean. But there's a lot more going on than the cliché suggests.</p>

<p>The Gangnam district covers several sub-neighborhoods — Apgujeong, Cheongdam-dong, Sinsa-dong (home of Garosu-gil), and the official Gangnam-gu administrative area. Each has a slightly different vibe, and together they form one of the most interesting urban landscapes in Asia.</p>

<h2>Getting to Gangnam</h2>
<p>Take Line 2 (green) to Gangnam Station, or Line 3 (orange) to Apgujeong Station, depending on where you're headed. The subway is fast and reliable — from central Seoul (Myeongdong area), it's about 20–25 minutes.</p>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> The Gangnam area is large — don't try to walk between Gangnam Station and Apgujeong. Use the subway or hail a taxi (Kakao T app is the easiest way to book; you can set the destination in English).</div>

<h2>Garosu-gil: The Most Stylish Street in Seoul</h2>
<p><strong>Garosu-gil</strong> (가로수길, "tree-lined street") runs through Sinsa-dong and is lined with ginkgo trees, indie boutiques, design studios, and some of the best cafés in the city. Unlike Myeongdong's chain-store commercialism, Garosu-gil leans heavily toward local designers and independent brands.</p>

<p>Start at Sinsa Station (Line 3, Exit 8) and walk north. You'll pass concept stores selling handmade jewelry, Korean streetwear labels, and minimalist home goods. The parallel streets ("Sero-gil" or the "back streets of Garosu-gil") are even more interesting — quieter, cheaper, and favored by locals over tourists.</p>

<h3>Best Cafés on Garosu-gil</h3>
<p><strong>Fritz Coffee</strong> — one of the most beloved specialty coffee roasters in Seoul, with a big dog logo and a loyal following. Expect a queue on weekends. <strong>Onion Apgujeong</strong> — a bakery-café in a converted old building, with sourdough that sells out by noon. <strong>Blue Bottle Coffee</strong> has a flagship here too, but honestly, the local spots are better.</p>

<h2>COEX Mall & Starfield Library</h2>
<p>The <strong>COEX Mall</strong> is the largest underground shopping mall in Korea, connected to a convention center, an aquarium, and a casino. It's worth visiting for the <strong>Starfield Library</strong> alone — a two-story open public library with shelves rising up to 13 meters, filled with 50,000 books and lined with reading chairs. It's free to enter and genuinely stunning to photograph.</p>

<p>The mall also has a good food hall in the basement, multiple cinema screens, and a branch of the K-pop flagship store SM Town. The COEX Aquarium (₩29,000) is decent but not essential unless you're traveling with kids.</p>

<h2>Apgujeong Rodeo Street</h2>
<p><strong>Apgujeong Rodeo Street</strong> is where the old money lives — luxury boutiques (Chanel, Prada, Bottega Veneta), high-end plastic surgery clinics, and the kind of restaurants where a lunch course costs ₩150,000 per person. It feels a bit like Beverly Hills transplanted to Korea, which is partly the point.</p>

<p>The area is also home to many K-pop entertainment agencies. <strong>SM Entertainment</strong>, <strong>JYP Entertainment</strong>, and <strong>YG Entertainment</strong> all have offices nearby. Fans frequently gather outside hoping to spot artists, which has become its own form of Seoul street theater.</p>

<h3>Cheongdam-dong: Seoul's Fashion District</h3>
<p>Just east of Apgujeong, <strong>Cheongdam-dong</strong> is where Korean fashion houses set up their flagship stores. Walk along Cheongdam-daero and you'll see both international luxury brands and Korean designers who are making serious waves globally — <strong>Blindness</strong>, <strong>Münn</strong>, <strong>Lie Sang Bong</strong>. The area also has some of the best fine dining in Seoul.</p>

<h2>Korean BBQ in Gangnam</h2>
<p>Gangnam has no shortage of excellent Korean BBQ, and the quality skews higher here than in most other neighborhoods. <strong>Maple Tree House</strong> (메이플트리하우스) in Itaewon-adjacent area is famous for its premium hanwoo beef. For a more accessible price point, the streets around Gangnam Station (especially behind Exit 10) are packed with good BBQ spots where you can get samgyeopsal (pork belly) for ₩15,000–18,000 per portion.</p>

<div class="tip-box">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> At Korean BBQ restaurants, the server usually grills the meat for you at upscale spots. At mid-range places, you do it yourself. Don't be shy about asking for more banchan (side dishes) — they're always free refills.</div>

<h2>Nightlife: Clubs and Rooftop Bars</h2>
<p>Gangnam's nightlife is more upscale than Hongdae — the clubs here cater to a slightly older, more affluent crowd, and dress codes are enforced. <strong>Octagon</strong> consistently ranks among Asia's top clubs and draws world-class DJs. <strong>Arena</strong> is another massive venue. Cover charges run ₩20,000–30,000 and include one drink.</p>

<p>For something more low-key, the rooftop bars around Gangnam Station offer decent cocktails and views of the city skyline at night. <strong>Charles H.</strong> at the Four Seasons in Gwanghwamun is technically not Gangnam but worth the mention for the best cocktail program in Seoul.</p>

<h2>Shopping: What to Buy in Gangnam</h2>
<p>Besides the international luxury brands, Gangnam is the place to buy Korean skincare and cosmetics from higher-end brands not found in Myeongdong. <strong>Amorepacific</strong>'s flagship store is here. The underground shopping arcades around Gangnam Station also have great deals on Korean fashion basics — t-shirts, trousers, and outerwear at reasonable prices.</p>

